The negative start pulse is strongest for lowercase alphabetic fragments,
particularly ones uncommon in German. Elsewhere the estimate is near zero,
with separate small pulses for message/tool boundaries and printed escapes.
Two conservative lookalike cues give positive pulses: a run of uppercase
I's after a lowercase Latin letter, or a Latin-looking Cyrillic letter
attached to Latin text. Short tab-prefixed fragments give a negative pulse.
These visible cues summarize repeated training patterns, but leave most
lexical variation unexplained.
